        .values = .{
            .text = "Proteomics Standards Initiative Mass Spectrometry Vocabularies.A reference number relevant to the sample under study.A reference string relevant to the sample under study.The chemical phase of a pure sample, or the state of a mixed sample.Total mass of sample used.Total volume of solution used.Concentration of sample in picomol/ul, femtomol/ul or attomol/ul solution used.The nature of the sample inlet.The method by which gas phase ions are generated from the sample.OBSOLETE Whether positive or negative ions are selected for analysis by the spectrometer.OBSOLETE The common name of the particular analyzer stage being described. Synonym of mass analyzer, should be obsoleted.Smallest mass difference between two equal magnitude peaks so that the valley between them is a specified fraction of the peak height.Which of the available standard measures is used to define whether two peaks are separate.OBSOLETE Specify the nature of resolution for the mass analyzer. Resolution is usually either constant with respect to m/z or proportional to m/z.Accuracy is the degree of conformity of a measured mass to its actual value.Rate in Th/sec for scanning analyzers.The time that an analyzer started a scan, relative to the start of the MS run.OBSOLETE Describes the type of mass analysis being performed. Two primary modes are: typical acquisition over a range of masses (Mass Scan), and Selected Ion Detection. The primary difference is that Selected Ion Detection produces a single value for the signal at the selected mass rather than producing a mass spectrum.Direction in terms of m/z of the scan for scanning analyzers (low to high, or high to low).Describes the function in control of the m/z scan (for scanning instruments). Commonly the scan function is linear, but in principle any function can be used.Describes the acquisition data type produced by a tandem mass spectrometry experiment.Status of the reflectron, turned on or off.The length of the field free drift space in a time of flight mass spectrometer.OBSOLETE The total width (i.e. not half for plus-or-minus) of the gate applied around a selected precursor ion.Final MS level achieved when performing PFF with the ion trap (e.g. MS E10).A property of space that produces a force on a charged particle equal to qv x B where q is the particle charge and v its velocity.Type of detector used in the mass spectrometer.Method by which detector signal is acquired by the data system.The resolving power of the detector to detect the smallest difference between two ions so that the valley between them is a specified fraction of the peak height.The rate of signal sampling (measurement) with respect to time.OBSOLETE Name of instrument vendor.Instrument model name not including the vendor's name.Free text description of a single customization made to the instrument; for several modifications, use several entries.The removal of isotope peaks to represent the ion as one data point and is commonly done to reduce complexity. It is done in conjunction with the charge state deconvolution.The determination of the mass of an ion based on the mass spectral peaks that represent multiple-charge ions.Spectral peak processing conducted on the acquired data to convert profile data to centroided data.OBSOLETE.OBSOLETE Terms to describe the polarity setting of the instrument.OBSOLETE Acquisition time in minutes.OBSOLETE Acquisition time in seconds.Three-character symbol m/z is used to denote the quantity formed by dividing the mass of an ion in unified atomic mass units by its charge number (regardless of sign). The symbol is written in italicized lower case letters with no spaces. Note 1: The term mass-to-charge-ratio is deprecated. Mass-to-charge ratio has been used for the abscissa of a mass spectrum, although the quantity measured is not the quotient of the ion's mass to its electric charge. The three-character symbol m/z is recommended for the quantity that is the independent variable in a mass spectrum Note 2: The proposed unit thomson (Th) is deprecated.Number of net charges, positive or negative, on an ion.Intensity of ions as measured by the height or area of a peak in a mass spectrum.Intensity units are commonly arbitrary. Detected in counts per second (cps) when using counting detectors, but measured in volts when using analog detectors.Fragmentation method used for dissociation or fragmentation.Energy for an ion experiencing collision with a stationary gas particle resulting in dissociation of the ion.OBSOLETE Energy units are represented in either eV or Joules.State if the sample is in emulsion form.State if the sample is in gaseous form.State if the sample is in liquid form.State if the sample is in solid form.State if the sample is in solution form.State if the sample is in suspension form.Sample batch lot identifier.OBSOLETE Chromatographic conditions used to obtain the sample.Fast atom bombardment ionization in which the analyte in solution is entrained in a flowing liquid matrix.The sample is directly inserted into the ion source, usually on the end of a heatable probe.Inlet used for introducing the liquid sample into an electrospray ionization source.A sample introduction method in which a discrete sample aliquot is injected into a continuous carrier solvent stream and transported to the ionization source without separation, producing a transient analytical signal.A gas discharge ion source in which the energy to the plasma is supplied by electromagnetic induction.A sample introduction method in which a sample solution is continuously delivered to the ionization source at a constant flow rate without separation.A device that separates carrier gas from gaseous analyte molecules on the basis of diffusivity.A device to separate carrier molecules from analyte molecules on the basis of ease of diffusion across a semipermeable membrane.Continuous moving surface in the form of a belt which passes through an ion source carrying analyte molecules.Continuous moving surface in the form of a wire which passes through an ion source carrying analyte molecules.A division of flowing stream of liquid into two streams.Method for generating ions from a solution of an analyte.A sample inlet method involving a reservoir.A disc composed of a flexible material that seals the entrance to the reservoir. Can also be entrance to the vacuum chamber.A method for generating gas phase ions from a solution of an analyte by rapid heating of the sample.Chemical ionization that takes place at atmospheric pressure as opposed to the reduced pressure is normally used for chemical ionization.The formation of a new ion by the reaction of a neutral species with an ion. The process may involve transfer of an electron, a proton or other charged species between the reactants. When a positive ion results from chemical ionization the term may be used without qualification. When a negative ion results the term negative ion chemical ionization should be used. Note that this term is not synonymous with chemi-ionization.OBSOLETE The ionization of an atom or molecule by electrons that are typically accelerated to energies between 50 and 150 eV. Usually 70 eV electrons are used to produce positive ions. The term 'electron impact' is not recommended.A process in which ionized species in the gas phase are produced from an analyte-containing solution via highly charged fine droplets, by means of spraying the solution from a narrow-bore needle tip at atmospheric pressure in the presence of a high electric field. When a pressurized gas is used to aid in the formation of a stable spray, the term pneumatically assisted electrospray ionization is used. The term ion spray is not recommended.The ionization of any species by the interaction of a focused beam of neutral atoms having a translational energy of several thousand eV with a sample that is typically dissolved in a solvent matrix. See also secondary ionization.The formation of gas-phase ions from molecules that are present in a solid or solvent matrix that is irradiated with a pulsed laser. See also laser desorption/ionization.Polarities of the scans of a run are all negative.Polarities of the scans of a run are all positive.A linear ion trap mass analyzer where ions are ejected along the axis of the analyzer.A device based on the principle of ion cyclotron resonance in which an ion in a magnetic field moves in a circular orbit at a frequency characteristic of its m/z value. Ions are coherently excited to a larger radius orbit using a pulse of radio frequency energy and their image charge is detected on receiver plates as a time domain signal. Fourier transformation of the time domain signal results in a frequency domain signal which is converted to a mass spectrum based in the inverse relationship between frequency and m/z.A device that produces a magnetic field perpendicular to a charged particle beam that deflects the beam to an extent that is proportional to the particle momentum per unit charge. For a monoenergetic beam, the deflection is proportional to m/z.A device that consists of four parallel rods whose centers form the corners of a square and whose opposing poles are connected. The voltage applied to the rods is a superposition of a static potential and a sinusoidal radio frequency potential. The motion of an ion in the x and y dimensions is described by the Matthieu equation whose solutions show that ions in a particular m/z range can be transmitted along the z axis.Quadrupole Ion Trap mass analyzer captures the ions in a three dimensional ion trap and then selectively ejects them by varying the RF and DC potentials.A linear ion trap mass analyzer where ions are ejected along the radius of the analyzer.Instrument that separates ions by m/z in a field-free region after acceleration to a fixed acceleration energy.An attribute of resolution when recording the detector response in absence of the analyte.A measure of resolution represented as width of the peak at half peak height.An attribute of resolution when the ratio between adjacent signals is 10% of the signal height.OBSOLETE When resolution is constant with respect to m/z.OBSOLETE When resolution is proportional with respect to m/z.OBSOLETE A variation of instrument where a selected mass is scanned.OBSOLETE Please see Single Ion Monitoring.High to low direction in terms of m/z of the scan for scanning analyzers.Low to high direction in terms of m/z of the scan for scanning analyzers.The mass scan is done in exponential mode.OBSOLETE The mass scan is done in linear mode.The mass scan is done in quadratic mode.OBSOLETE A spectrum formed of all product ions that have been produced with a selected m/z decrement from any precursor ions. The spectrum shown correlates to the precursor ion spectrum. See also neutral loss spectrum.OBSOLETE Data acquired when monitoring the ion current of a few specific m/z values. Remap to MS:1000205 -Selected Ion Monitoring.OBSOLETE This term is not recommended. See Selected Reaction Monitoring.OBSOLETE The specific scan function or process that will record a precursor ion spectrum.OBSOLETE The specific scan function or process that records product ion spectrum.OBSOLETE The operation of a mass spectrometer to monitor a single ion rather than scanning entire mass spectrum.OBSOLETE This term is not recommended. See Selected Reaction Monitoring.OBSOLETE None.Reflectron is off.Reflectron is on.A horn-shaped (or cone-shaped) continuous dynode particle multiplier. The ion strikes the inner surface of the device and induces the production of secondary electrons that in turn impinge on the inner surfaces to produce more secondary electrons. This avalanche effect produces an increase in signal in the final measured current pulse.A surface that is held at high potential so that ions striking the surface produce electrons that are subsequently detected.A detector in which ions strike a conversion dynode to produce electrons that in turn generate photons through a phosphorescent screen that are detected by a photomultiplier.Detector consisting of a conversion dynode, scintillator and photomultiplier. The metal knob at high potential emits secondary electrons when ions impinge on the surface. The secondary electrons are accelerated onto the scintillator that produces light that is then detected by the photomultiplier detector.A device to amplify the current of a beam or packet of charged particles or photons by incidence upon the surface of an electrode to produce secondary electrons.A conducting cup or chamber that intercepts a charged particle beam and is electrically connected to a current measuring device.An array of detectors for spatially disperse ion beams in which all ions simultaneously impinge on the detector plane.A thin plate that contains a closely spaced array of channels that each act as a continuous dynode particle multiplier. A charged particle, fast neutral particle, or photon striking the plate causes a cascade of secondary electrons that ultimately exits the opposite side of the plate.A detector system commonly used in inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometers.A detector for conversion of the ion/electron signal into photon(s) which are then amplified and detected.Analog-to-digital converter (abbreviated ADC, A/D or A to D) is an electronic integrated circuit (i/c) that converts continuous signals to discrete digital numbers.Definition to do.A device for converting a signal of sporadic pluses into a digital representation of their time indices.A detector acquisition mode used for detecting transient signals.SCIEX instrument model.Bruker Daltonics' instrument model.IonSpec corporation instrument model.Shimadzu corporation instrument model.ThermoFinnigan from Thermo Electron Corporation instrument model.Waters Corporation instrument model.A spectrum representation in which each peak is recorded as a single discrete data point at a distinct coordinate value (e.g. m/z; wavelength; drift time) with a corresponding intensity.A spectrum representation in which ion signal for each peak is recorded as a distribution of intensity values across the coordinate range (e.g. m/z; wavelength; drift time).Polarity of the scan is negative.Polarity of the scan is positive.The number of counted events observed in one or a group of elements of a detector.The magnitude of a peak or measurement element expressed in terms of the percentage of the magnitude of the base peak intensity.The dissociation of an ion after collisional excitation. The term collisional-activated dissociation is not recommended.The ionization of material in a solid sample by bombarding it with ionic or neutral atoms formed as a result of the fission of a suitable nuclide, typically 252Cf. Synonymous with fission fragment ionization.A technique specific to reflectron time-of-flight mass spectrometers where product ions of metastable transitions or collision-induced dissociations generated in the drift tube prior to entering the reflectron are m/z separated to yield product ion spectra.Fragmentation that results from the collision of an ion with a surface.OBSOLETE A non-SI unit of energy (eV) defined as the energy acquired by a particle containing one unit of charge through a potential difference of one volt. An electron-volt is equal to 1.602 176 53(14) x 10^-19 J.Instrument setting, expressed in percent, for adjusting collisional energies of ions in an effort to provide equivalent excitation of all ions.Applied Biosystems/MDS SCIEX Q 4000 TRAP MS.Applied Biosystems/MDS SCIEX 4700 Proteomics Analyzer MS.Bruker Daltonics' apex IV: ESI, MALDI, Nanospray, APCI, APPI, Qh-FT_ICR.Bruker Daltonics' apex Q: ESI, MALDI, Nanospray, APCI, APPI, Qh-FT_ICR.Applied Biosystems/MDS SCIEX API 150EX MS.Applied Biosystems/MDS SCIEX API 150EX Prep MS.Applied Biosystems/MDS SCIEX API 2000 MS.Applied Biosystems/MDS SCIEX API 3000 MS.Applied Biosystems/MDS SCIEX API 4000 MS.Bruker Daltonics' autoflex II: MALDI TOF.Bruker Daltonics' autoflex TOF/TOF MS: MALDI TOF.Waters magnetic sector based AutoSpec Ultima NT MS.Bruker Daltonics' BioTOF II: ESI TOF.Bruker Daltonics' BioTOF-Q: ESI Q-TOF.ThermoFinnigan DELTA plusAdvantage MS.ThermoFinnigan DELTAplusXP MS.OBSOLETE ThermoFinnigan ELEMENT2 MS.Bruker Daltonics' esquire 4000: linear ion trap, ESI, MALDI, Nanospray, APCI, APPI.Bruker Daltonics' esquire 6000: linear ion trap, ESI, MALDI, Nanospray, APCI, APPI.IonSpec Explorer MS.Waters oa-ToF based GCT.Bruker Daltonics' HCT: ESI Q-TOF, Nanospray, APCI, APPI.Bruker Daltonics' HCTplus: ESI Q-TOF, Nanospray, APCI, APPI.IonSpec HiResESI MS.IonSpec HiResMALDI MS.Waters IsoPrime MS.Waters IsoProbe MS.Waters IsoProbe T MS.ThermoFinnigan LCQ Advantage MS.ThermoFinnigan LCQ Classic MS.ThermoFinnigan LCQ Deca XP Plus MS.Waters oa-ToF based MALDI L.Waters oa-ToF based MALDI LR.ThermoFinnigan MAT253 MS.ThermoFinnigan MAT900XP MS.ThermoFinnigan MAT900XP Trap MS.ThermoFinnigan MAT95XP MS.ThermoFinnigan MAT95XP Trap MS.Bruker Daltonics' microflex: MALDI TOF.Bruker Daltonics' microTOF LC: ESI TOF, Nanospray, APCI, APPI.ThermoFinnigan NEPTUNE MS.Waters NG-5400 MS.IonSpec OMEGA MS.IonSpec OMEGA-2001 MS.Bruker Daltonics' OmniFlex: MALDI TOF.Waters Platform ICP MS.ThermoFinnigan PolarisQ MS.Applied Biosystems/MDS SCIEX Proteomics Solution 1 MS.Applied Biosystems/MDS SCIEX Q TRAP MS.Waters oa-ToF based Q-Tof micro.Waters oa-ToF based Q-Tof Ultima.Applied Biosystems/MDS SCIEX QSTAR MS.Waters (triple) quadrupole based micro.Waters (triple) quadrupole based Ultima.ThermoFinnigan Surveyor MSQ MS.Applied Biosystems/MDS SCIEX SymBiot I MS.Applied Biosystems/MDS SCIEX SymBiot XVI MS.ThermoFinnigan TEMPUS TOF MS.ThermoFinnigan TRACE DSQ MS.ThermoFinnigan TRITON MS.ThermoFinnigan TSQ Quantum MS.IonSpec Ultima MS.Bruker Daltonics' ultraflex: MALDI TOF.Bruker Daltonics' ultraflex TOF/TOF: MALDI TOF.Applied Biosystems/MDS SCIEX Voyager-DE PRO MS.Applied Biosystems/MDS SCIEX Voyager-DE STR MS.The operation of a mass spectrometer in which the intensities of several specific m/z values are recorded rather than the entire mass spectrum.Data acquired from specific product ions corresponding to m/z selected precursor ions recorded via multiple stages of mass spectrometry. Selected reaction monitoring can be performed in time or in space.OBSOLETE An experimentally determined mass that is can be to determine a unique elemental formula. For ions less than 200 u, a measurement with 5 ppm accuracy is sufficient to determine the elemental composition.OBSOLETE The mass of an ion or molecule calculated using the average mass of each element weighted for its natural isotopic abundance.OBSOLETE The minimum energy that must be imparted to an atom or molecule to produce a specified ion. The term appearance potential is not recommended.The peak in a mass spectrum that has the greatest intensity. This term may be applied to the spectra of pure substances or mixtures.OBSOLETE The total charge on an ion divided by the electron charge e. OBSOLETED 2009-10-27 since this was viewed as a duplication of 00041 charge state.OBSOLETE A non-SI unit of mass (symbol Da) that is equal to the unified atomic mass unit: 1.660 538 86(28) x 10^-27 kg.OBSOLETE The electron affinity of M is the minimum energy required for the process M- ? M + e where M- and M are in their ground rotational, vibrational and electronic states and the electron has zero kinetic energy.OBSOLETE The potential difference through which electrons are accelerated before they are used to bring about electron ionization.OBSOLETE The calculated mass of an ion or molecule containing a single isotope of each atom.A section of a mass spectrometer in which there are no electric or magnetic fields.OBSOLETE A measure of the probability that a given ionization process will occur when an atom or molecule interacts with a photon, electron, atom or molecule.OBSOLETE The ratio of the number of ions formed to the number of electrons, molecules or photons used.OBSOLETE The minimum energy required to remove an electron from an atom or molecule to produce a positive ion.OBSOLETE A quantitative mass spectrometry technique in which an isotopically enriched compound is used as an internal standard.The deflection of charged particles in a magnetic field due to a force equal to qvxB where q is the particle charge, v its velocity and B the magnetic field. Magnetic deflection of an ion beam is used for m/z separation in a magnetic sector mass spectrometer.OBSOLETE The difference between the monoisotopic and nominal mass of a molecule or atom.OBSOLETE The sum of the protons and neutrons in an atom, molecule or ion.Mass of a molecule measured in unified atomic mass units (u or Da).OBSOLETE The mass of an ion or molecule calculated using the mass of the most abundant isotope of each element.OBSOLETE A mass spectrometry technique in which the sample is introduced into the mass spectrometer as a molecular beam.Photoionization of an atom or molecule in which in two or more photons are absorbed.OBSOLETE An organic molecule containing the elements C, H, O, S, P, or halogen has an odd nominal mass if it contains an odd number of nitrogen atoms.OBSOLETE The mass of an ion or molecule calculated using the mass of the most abundant isotope of each element rounded to the nearest integer value.OBSOLETE Odd-electron ions may dissociate to form either odd or even-electron ions, whereas even-electron ions generally form even-electron fragment ions.A localized region of relatively large ion signal in a mass spectrum. Although peaks are often associated with particular ions, the terms peak and ion should not be used interchangeably.OBSOLETE The height or area of a peak in a mass spectrum.OBSOLETE The proton affinity of a species M is defined as the negative of the enthalpy change for the reaction M + H+ ->[M+H]+, where all species are in their ground rotational, vibrational and electronic states.OBSOLETE In a mass spectrum, the observed mass divided by the difference between two masses that can be separated. The method by which delta m was obtained and the mass at which the measurement was made should be reported.Representation of the total ion current detected in each of a series of mass spectra versus time.The ratio of the number of ions leaving a region of a mass spectrometer to the number entering that region.OBSOLETE A non-SI unit of mass (u) defined as one twelfth of ^12 C in its ground state and equal to 1.660 538 86(28) x 10^-27 kg.OBSOLETE A mass spectrometry technique in which atoms extracted from a sample are ionized, accelerated to MeV energies and separated according to their momentum, charge and energy.Matrix-assisted laser desorption ionization in which the sample target is at atmospheric pressure and the ions formed by the pulsed laser are sampled through a small aperture into the mass spectrometer.Any ionization process in which ions are formed in the gas phase at atmospheric pressure.OBSOLETE Atmospheric pressure chemical ionization in which the reactant ions are generated by photo-ionization.A special case of infrared multiphoton dissociation wherein excitation of the reactant ion is caused by absorption of infrared photons radiating from heated blackbody surroundings, which are usually the walls of a vacuum chamber. See also infrared multiphoton dissociation.OBSOLETE A fragmentation of an even-electron ion in which the cleaved bond is not adjacent to the apparent charge site.OBSOLETE MSn experiment with three or more stages of m/z separation and in which a particular multi-step reaction path is monitored.The reaction of a positive ion with an atom or molecule that results in the removal of one or more electrons from the ion.The application of the accelerating voltage pulse after a time delay in desorption ionization from a surface. The extraction delay can produce energy focusing in a time-of-flight mass spectrometer.The formation of ions from a solid or liquid material after the rapid vaporization of that sample.A device for introducing a solid or liquid sample into a mass spectrometer ion source for desorption ionization.A legacy liquid chromatography-mass spectrometry interface in which a minor fraction of the eluate flow is split and introduced through a narrow orifice or diaphragm directly into the ion source, with solvent-mediated chemical ionization. This interface was largely superseded by atmospheric pressure ionization methods.A process in which a multiply protonated molecules interacts with a low energy electrons. Capture of the electron leads the liberation of energy and a reduction in charge state of the ion with the production of the (M + nH) (n-1)+ odd electron ion, which readily fragments.OBSOLETE An ion containing no unpaired electrons in its ground electronic state, e.g. CH3+ in its ground state.OBSOLETE The reaction of an ion with an electron in which the translational energy of the collision is converted into internal energy of the ion.A device to amplify the current of a beam or packet of charged particles or photons by incidence upon the surface of an electrode to produce secondary electrons. The secondary electrons are then accelerated to other electrodes or parts of a continuous electrode to produce further secondary electrons.A device consisting of conducting parallel plates, concentric cylinders or concentric spheres that separates charged particles according to their kinetic energy by means of an electric field that is constant in time.An ion source immersed in a flow of helium or other inert buffer gas that carries the ions through a meter-long reactor at pressures around 100 Pa.OBSOLETE The separation of ions between two concentric cylindrical electrodes due to application of a high voltage asymmetric waveform whereby ions migrate towards one of the two electrodes depending on the ratio of the high- to low-field mobility of the ion.The formation of gas-phase ions from a material deposited on a solid surface in the presence of a high electric field. Because this process may encompass ionization by field ionization or other mechanisms, it is not recommended as a synonym for field desorption ionization.The removal of electrons from any species by interaction with a high electric field.The formation of ions in the gas phase and from solid samples at the cathode by application of a voltage to a low pressure gas.OBSOLETE A method of analysis in which a beam of ions is separated according to the ratio of its translational energy to charge.OBSOLETE The separation of ions according to their velocity through a buffer gas under the influence of an electric field.Multiphoton ionization where the reactant ion dissociates as a result of the absorption of multiple infrared photons.OBSOLETE The measurement of the relative quantity of the different isotopes of an element in a material with a mass spectrometer.A device for spatially confining ions using electric and magnetic fields alone or in combination.OBSOLETE Distribution of values of translational kinetic energy release for an ensemble of metastable ions undergoing a specific dissociation reaction.OBSOLETE The formation of ions through the interaction of a laser with a material or with gas-phase ions or molecules.OBSOLETE Spectra that are obtained from a sector mass spectrometer that incorporates at least one magnetic sector plus one electric sector in reverse geometry. The accelerating voltage, V, and the magnetic sector field, B, are set at fixed values to select the precursor ions, which are then allowed to dissociate or to react in a field free region between the two sectors. The kinetic energy product ions of m/z selected precursor ions is analyzed by scanning the electric sector field, E. The width of the product ion spectrum peaks is related to the kinetic energy release distribution (KERD) for the dissociation process.OBSOLETE The branch of science that deals with all aspects of mass spectrometers and the results obtained with these instruments.OBSOLETE The acquisition, study and spectra of the electrically charged products or precursors of a m/z selected ion or ions.OBSOLETE Multiple stages of precursor ion m/z selection followed by product ion detection for successive progeny ions.Chemical ionization that results in the formation of negative ions.With this technique, m/z selected ions form neutrals by charge transfer to a collision gas or by dissociation. The neutrals are separated from the remaining ions and ionized in collisions with a second gas. This method is used to investigate reaction intermediates and other unstable species.The ionization of an atom or molecule by a photon, written M + h? ? M^+ + e. The term photon impact is not recommended.A mass spectrometry technique in which the sample is heated to the point of decomposition and the gaseous decomposition products are introduced into the ion source.A transmission quadrupole to which an oscillating potential is applied so as to focus a beam of ions through a collision gas with no m/z separation.Multiphoton ionization in which the ionization cross section is significantly enhanced because the energy of the incident photons is resonant with an intermediate excited state of the neutral species.OBSOLETE A mass spectrometer used to measure the composition and pressure of gasses in an evacuated chamber.The formation of ionized species in the gas phase from analytes deposited on a particular surface substrate which is irradiated with a laser beam of which wavelength is absorbed by the surface. See also desorption/ionization on silicon and laser desorption/ionization.Matrix-assisted laser desorption ionization in which the matrix is covalently linked to the target surface.OBSOLETE The ionization of a neutral species when it interacts with a solid surface with an appropriate work function and temperature.A device in which m/z selected ions are entrained in an inert carrier gas and undergo ion-molecule reactions.A technique associated with Fourier transform ion cyclotron resonance (FT-ICR) mass spectrometry to carry out ion/neutral reactions such as low-energy collision-induced dissociation. A radio-frequency electric field of slightly off-resonance to the cyclotron frequency of the reactant ion cyclically accelerates and decelerates the reactant ion that is confined in the Penning ion trap. The ion's orbit does not exceed the dimensions of ion trap while the ion undergoes an ion/neutral species process that produces a high average translational energy for an extended time.OBSOLETE Mass spectrometry using spark ionization.A technique to create excitation waveforms for ions in FT-ICR mass spectrometer or Paul ion trap. An excitation waveform in the time-domain is generated by taking the inverse Fourier transform of an appropriate frequency-domain programmed excitation spectrum, in which the resonance frequencies of ions to be excited are included. This technique may be used for selection of precursor ions in MS2 experiments.The sum of all the separate ion currents carried by the ions of different m/z contributing to a complete mass spectrum or in a specified m/z range of a mass spectrum.Energy focusing in a time-of-flight mass spectrometer that is accomplished by introducing a time delay between the formation of the ions and the application of the accelerating voltage pulse.OBSOLETE An instrument that separates ions by m/z in a field-free region after acceleration to a fixed kinetic energy.A device that uses an oscillating electric field and magnetic field to accelerate charged particles.OBSOLETE A mass spectrometer that uses a magnetic sector for m/z focusing and an electric sector for energy focusing of an ion beam.OBSOLETE A mass spectrometer that combines m/z analyzers of different types to perform tandem mass spectrometry.A two dimensional Paul ion trap in which ions are confined in the axial dimension by means of an electric field at the ends of the trap.OBSOLETE An instrument that separates a beam of ions according to their mass-to-charge ratio in which the ions are directed onto a focal plane detector such as a photographic plate.OBSOLETE An instrument that measures the mass-to-charge ratio and relative abundances of ions.A plot of the relative abundance of a beam or other collection of ions as a function of the mass-to-charge ratio (m/z).OBSOLETE An arrangement for a double-focusing mass spectrometer in which a deflection of ?/(4 ?(2)) radians in a radial electric field is followed by a magnetic deflection of ?/2 radians.OBSOLETE An arrangement for a double-focusing mass spectrometer in which a deflection of ?/2 radians in a radial electric field analyzer is followed by a magnetic deflection of ?/3 radians.OBSOLETE A device that permits the trapping of ions by means of an alternating current voltage. The ejection of ions with a m/z less than a prescribed value and retention of those with higher mass depends on the application of radio frequency voltages between a ring electrode and two end-cap electrodes to confine the ions in a circular path. The choice of these voltages determines the m/z below which ions are ejected.OBSOLETE A mass spectrometer in which the ions of different m/z are separated by means of crossed electric and magnetic fields in such a way that the selected ions follow a prolate trochoidal path.OBSOLETE An abbreviation of quadrupole ion storage trap. This term is synonymous with Paul Ion Trap. If so then add a synonym to paul and obsolete this term.A time-of-flight mass spectrometer that uses a static electric field to reverse the direction of travel of the ions entering it. A reflectron improves mass resolution by assuring that ions of the same m/z but different kinetic energy arrive at the detector at the same time.OBSOLETE A mass spectrometer consisting of one or more magnetic sectors for m/z selection in a beam of ions. Such instruments may also have one or more electric sectors for energy selection.OBSOLETE A mass spectrometer designed for mass spectrometry/mass spectrometry.OBSOLETE A mass spectrometer that consists of four parallel rods whose centers form the corners of a square and whose opposing poles are connected. The voltage applied to the rods is a superposition of a static potential and a sinusoidal radio frequency potential. The motion of an ion in the x and y dimensions is described by the Matthieu equation whose solutions show that ions in a particular m/z range can be transmitted along the z axis.The electrical potential used to impart kinetic energy to ions in a mass spectrometer.OBSOLETE The circular motion of a charged particle moving at velocity v in a magnetic field B that results from the force qvB.OBSOLETE A mass spectrometer in which m/z separation using one or more electric fields that vary with time.Three element charged particle lens in which the first and third elements are held at the same voltage. Such a lens produces focusing without changing the translational energy of the particle.The magnitude of the force per unit charge at a given point in space.The region of a Mathieu stability diagram closest to the origin. Ions within this region can traverse the full length of a transmission quadrupole.The electric or magnetic field that extends from the edge of a sector, lens or other ion optics element.A device for measuring the kinetic energy of charged particles using a retarding field, time-of-flight, or the extent of deflection in an electric or magnetic field.OBSOLETE The m/z value above which ions cannot be detected in a mass spectrometer.OBSOLETE The limit of m/z over which a mass spectrometer can detect ions.OBSOLETE The use of mass selective instability to eject ions of selected m/z values from an ion trap.OBSOLETE A method for selective ejection of ions according to their m/z value in an ion trap.OBSOLETE A graphical representation expressed in terms of reduced coordinates that describes charged particle motion in a quadrupole mass filter or quadrupole ion trap mass spectrometer.OBSOLETE The pulsed acceleration of ions perpendicular to their direction of travel into a time-of-flight mass spectrometer. Ions may be extracted from a directional ion source, drift tube or m/z separation stage.OBSOLETE A mode of ion ejection in a quadrupole ion trap that relies on a auxiliary radio frequency voltage that is applied to the end-cap electrodes. The voltage is tuned to the secular frequency of a particular ion to eject it.The mutual repulsion of particles of like charge that limits the current in a charged-particle beam and causes beams or packets of charged particles to expand radially over time.An electric or magnetic field that does not change in time.OBSOLETE A mass spectrum obtained by setting the electric sector field E to twice the value required to transmit the main ion-beam thereby allowing ions with a kinetic energy-to-charge ratio twice that of the main ion-beam to be transmitted. Product ions resulting from partial charge transfer reactions such as m^2+ + N ? m^+ + N^+ that occur in a collision cell (containing a gas, N) located in a field-free region preceding a magnetic and electric sector combination are detected. When the magnetic sector field B is scanned, a mass spectrum of singly charged product ions of doubly charged precursor ions is obtained.The measurement of the relative abundance of ions that result from a charge inversion reaction as a function of m/z.OBSOLETE Spectrum of all precursor ions that undergo a selected m/z decrement.OBSOLETE Spectrum of all precursor ions that undergo a selected m/z increment.A spectrum formed of all product ions that have been produced by gain of a pre-selected neutral mass following the reaction with and addition of the gas in a collision cell.A spectrum formed of all product ions that have been produced with a selected m/z decrement from any precursor ions. The spectrum shown correlates to the precursor ion spectrum. See also neutral loss spectrum.OBSOLETE A type of MS2 experiments with three or more stages of m/z separation and in which a particular multi-step reaction path is monitored.A mass spectrum obtained using a sector mass spectrometer in which the electric sector field E is set to half the value required to transmit the main ion-beam. This spectrum records the signal from doubly charged product ions of charge-stripping reactions.OBSOLETE A scan in an instrument with two or more m/z analysers or in a sector mass spectrometer that incorporates at least one magnetic sector and one electric sector. Two or more of the analyzers are scanned simultaneously so as to preserve a predetermined relationship between scan parameters to produce a product ion, precursor ion or constant neutral loss spectrum.OBSOLETE A linked scan at constant B/E may be performed on a sector mass spectrometer that incorporates at least one magnetic sector plus one electric sector. The magnetic field B and the electric field E are scanned simultaneously while the accelerating voltage V is held constant, so as to maintain the ratio of the two fields constant. This linked scan may record a product ion spectrum of dissociation or other reactions occurring in a field free region preceding the two sectors.OBSOLETE A linked scan performed on a sector instrument that incorporates at least one electric sector plus one magnetic sector. The electric sector field, E, and the accelerating voltage, V, are scanned simultaneously, so as to maintain the ratio E2/V at a constant value. This linked scan records a product ion spectrum of dissociation or other reactions occurring in a field free region (FFR) preceding the two sectors.OBSOLETE A linked scan performed on a sector mass spectrometer that incorporates at least one electric sector plus one magnetic sector in either order. The accelerating voltage is fixed and the magnetic field, B, and the electric field, E, are scanned simultaneously so as to maintain the ratio B2/E at a constant value. This linked scan records a precursor ion spectrum of dissociation or other reactions occurring in the field free region preceding the two sectors. The term B2/E linked scan is not recommended.OBSOLETE A linked scan performed on a sector instrument that incorporates at least one electric sector plus one magnetic sector placed in either order. The accelerating voltage is fixed while scanning the magnetic field, B, and electric field, E, simultaneously, so as to maintain the quantity B[1-(E/E0)]1/2/E at a constant value. This linked scan records a constant neutral mass loss (or gain) spectrum of dissociation or other reactions occurring in a field free region preceding the two sectors. E0 is the electric field required to transmit the singly charged analog of the desired neutral fragment. The term B[1-(E/E0)]1/2/E linked scan.OBSOLETE A tandem mass spectrometry method in which product ion spectra are recorded in a single m/z analyzer (such as a Paul Ion Trap or FTMS) in discrete steps over time. Ions in a specific m/z range are selected, dissociated, and the product ions analyzed sequentially in time.OBSOLETE A tandem mass spectrometry method in which product ion spectra are recorded in m/z analyzers separated in space. Specific m/z separation functions are designed such that in one section of the instrument ions are selected, dissociated in an intermediate region, and the product ions are then transmitted to another analyser for m/z separation and data acquisition.The loss of an uncharged species during a rearrangement process. The value slot holds the molecular formula in Hill notation of the neutral loss molecule, see PMID:21182243. This term must be used in conjunction with a child of the term MS:1002307 (fragmentation ion type).OBSOLETE Serial product ions from dissociation of selected precursor ions where n refers to the number of stages of dissociation. The term granddaughter ion is deprecated.OBSOLETE The specific scan functions or processes that record the appropriate generation of product ion or ions of any m/z selected precursor ions.OBSOLETE The mass spectrum recorded from any mass spectrometer in which the appropriate scan function can be set to record the appropriate generation product ion or ions of m/z selected precursor ions.OBSOLETE An ion that reacts to form particular product ions. The reaction can be unimolecular dissociation, ion/molecule reaction, isomerization, or change in charge state. The term parent ion is deprecated.Spectrum generated by scanning precursor m/z while monitoring a fixed product m/z.OBSOLETE An ion formed as the product of a reaction involving a particular precursor ion. The reaction can be unimolecular dissociation to form fragment ions, an ion/molecule reaction, or simply involve a change in the number of charges. The term fragment ion is deprecated. The term daughter ion is deprecated.OBSOLETE A mass spectrum recorded from any spectrometer in which the appropriate m/z separation scan function is set to record the product ion or ions of selected precursor ions.OBSOLETE A charged product of a series of consecutive reactions that includes product ions, 1st generation product ions, 2nd generation product ions, etc. Given the sequential fragmentation scheme: M1+ -> M2+ -> M3+ -> M4+ -> M5+. M4+ is the precursor ion of M5+, a 1st generation product ion of M3+, a 2nd generation product ion of M2+ and a 3rd generation product ion of M1+.Detector comprising several ion collection elements, arranged in a line or grid where each element is an individual detector.A surface that is held at high potential such that ions striking the surface produce electrons that are subsequently detected.One of a series of electrodes in a photomultiplier tube. Such an arrangement is able to amplify the current emitted by the photocathode.A detector for spatially disperse ion beams in which all ions simultaneously impinge on the detector plane.A detector in which ions strike a conversion dynode to produce electrons that in turn strike a phosphor and the resulting photons are detected by a photomultiplier.A detector in which the ion beam is focused onto a point and the individual ions arrive sequentially.A detector in which the charged particles are accelerated to a high velocity and impinge on a conversion dynode, emitting secondary electrons. The electrons are accelerated onto a phosphor screen, which emits photons that are in turn detected using a photomultiplier or other photon detector.OBSOLETE Electrons that are ejected from a sample surface as a result of bombardment by a primary beam of atoms, ions or photons. WAS IN DETECTOR TYPE. Where should it go.Ion formed by the interaction of an ion with one or more atoms or molecules to form an ion containing all the constituent atoms of the precursor ion as well as the additional atoms from the associated atoms or molecules.OBSOLETE A planar cyclic ion that obeys the Hueckel (4n + 2) rule where n is a positive integer representing the number of conjugated Pi electrons. Charge delocalization leads to greater stability compared to a hypothetical localized structure.OBSOLETE Ions that have similar chemical valence, for example the acetyl cation CH3-CO+ and the thioacetyl cation CH3-CS+.OBSOLETE A planar cyclic ion with 4n ? electrons and is therefore not aromatic.OBSOLETE An ion formed by the association of a cation with a neutral molecule, M, for example [M+ Na]+ and [M + K]+. The terms quasi-molecular ion and pseudo-molecular ion should not be used.OBSOLETE An ion formed by a multi-component atomic or molecular assembly of one or more ions with atoms or molecules, such as [(H20)nH]+, [(NaCl)nNa]+ and [(H3PO3)nHPO3]-.OBSOLETE A radical cation or anion in which the charge site and the unpaired electron spin are both formally located in the same atom or group of atoms, as opposed to the spatially separate electronic configuration of distonic ions. The radical cation of methanol, CH3OH+, in which the charge and spin sites are formally located at the O atom is an example of a conventional ion, whereas .CH2-OH2+ is a distonic ion.OBSOLETE A product ion whose formation reveals structural or compositional information of its precursor. For instance, the phenyl cation in an electron ionization mass spectrum is a diagnostic ion for benzene and derivatives.OBSOLETE An ion formed by ionization of a dimer or by the association of an ion with its neutral counterpart such as [M2]+ or [M-H-M]+.OBSOLETE A radical cation or anion in which the charge site and the unpaired electron spin cannot be both formally located in the same atom or group of atoms as it can be with a conventional ion. For example, CH2-OH2+ is a distonic ion whereas the radical cation of methanol, CH3OH+ is a conventional ion.OBSOLETE A positively charged lower-valency ion of the nonmetallic elements. The methenium ion is CH3+. Other examples are the oxenium, sulfenium, nitrenium, phosphenium, and halenium ions.OBSOLETE A product ion that results from the dissociation of a precursor ion.OBSOLETE An atomic or molecular species having a net positive or negative electric charge.OBSOLETE An ion that differs only in the isotopic composition of one or more of its constituent atoms. For example CH4+ and CH3D+ or 10BF3 and 11BF3. The term isotopologue is a contraction of isotopic homologue.OBSOLETE Isomeric ion having the same numbers of each isotopic atom but differing in their positions. Isotopomeric ions can be either configurational isomers in which two atomic isotopes exchange positions or isotopic stereoisomers. The term isotopomer is a shortening of isotopic isomer.OBSOLETE An ion that is formed with internal energy higher than the threshold for dissociation but with a lifetime great enough to allow it to exit the ion source and enter the mass spectrometer where it dissociates before detection.OBSOLETE An ion formed by the removal of one or more electrons to form a positive ion or the addition off one or more electrons to form a negative ion.OBSOLETE An atomic or molecular species having a net negative electric charge.OBSOLETE Hyper-coordinated carbonium ion such as the penta-coordinated norbornyl cation. Note: Tri-coordinated carbenium ions are termed classical ions.OBSOLETE A positively charged hypervalent ion of the nonmetallic elements. Examples are the methonium ion CH5+, the hydrogenonium ion H3+ and the hydronium ion H3O+. Other examples are the carbonium, oxonium, sulfonium, nitronium, diazonium, phosphonium, and halonium ions. Onium ions are not limited to monopositive ions; multiply-charged onium ions exist such as the gitonic (proximal) oxonium dication H4O2+ and the distonic oxonium dication H2O+-CH2-CH2-OH2+.OBSOLETE Most abundant ion of an isotope cluster, such as the 11B79Br2 81Br+ ion of m/z 250 of the cluster of isotopologue molecular ions of BBr3. The term principal ion has also been used to describe ions that have been artificially isotopically enriched in one or more positions such as CH3 13CH3+ or CH2D2 +, but those are best defined as isotopologue ions.OBSOLETE An atomic or molecular species having a net positive electric charge.OBSOLETE An ion formed by interaction of a neutral molecule with a proton and represented by the symbol [M + H]+, where M is the neutral molecule. The term 'protonated molecular ion,' 'quasi-molecular ion' and 'pseudo-molecular ion' are not recommended.OBSOLETE An ion, either a cation or anion, containing unpaired electrons in its ground state. The unpaired electron is denoted by a superscript dot alongside the superscript symbol for charge, such as for the molecular ion of a molecule M, that is, M+. Radical ions with more than one charge and/or more than one unpaired electron are denoted such as M(2+)(2). Unless the positions of the unpaired electron and charge can be associated with specific atoms, superscript charge designation should be placed before the superscript dot designation.OBSOLETE A stable ion whose structure is known with certainty. These ions are usually formed by direct ionization of a neutral molecule of known structure and are used to verify by comparison the structure of an unknown ion.OBSOLETE An ion with internal energy sufficiently low that it does not rearrange or dissociate prior to detection in a mass spectrometer.OBSOLETE An ion with sufficient energy to dissociate within the ion source.A process whereby an electron is removed from an atom, ion, or molecule to produce an ion in its lowest energy state.An ionization process in which two excited atoms or molecules react to form a single positive ion and an electron.Atmospheric pressure chemical ionization in which the reactant ions are generated by photo-ionization.The formation of a neutral when a negative ion in a discrete state with an energy greater than the detachment threshold loses an electron spontaneously without further interaction with an energy source.The formation of an ion when an atom or molecule in a discrete state with an energy greater than the ionization threshold loses an electron spontaneously without further interaction with an energy source.The interaction of an ion with an atom or molecule in which the charge on the ion is transferred to the neutral without the dissociation of either. Synonymous with charge transfer ionization.The reaction of a neutral molecule with an internally excited molecule to form an ion. Note that this term is not synonymous with chemical ionization.The formation of ions by laser desorption ionization of a sample deposited on a porous silicon surface.The reaction of a gas-phase molecule that results in its decomposition to form products, one of which is an ion.The ionization of an atom or molecule by electrons that are typically accelerated to energies between 50 and 150 eV. Usually 70 eV electrons are used to produce positive ions. The term 'electron impact' is not recommended.OBSOLETE The removal of solvent molecules clustered around a gas-phase ion by means of heating and/or collisions with gas molecules.OBSOLETE The reaction of a molecule to form both a positive ion and negative ion fragment among the products.The ratio of the number of ions formed to the number of electrons, molecules or photons used.The formation of gas-phase ions by the interaction of a pulsed laser with a solid or liquid material.No reference sequence database was used in the search process to determine the identified peptide sequence, for example as with de novo sequencing.The ionization of any species by the interaction of a focused beam of ions with a sample that is dissolved in a solvent matrix. See also fast atom bombardment and secondary ionization.A semi-permeable membrane separator that permits the passage of gas sample directly to the mass spectrometer ion source.Electrospray ionization at a solvent flow rate of 300-800 nL/min where the flow is a result of a mechanical pump. See nanoelectrospray.Electrospray ionization at a flow rate less than ~25 nL/min. Nanoelectrospray is synonymous with nanospray. The flow is dependent on the potential on the tip of the electrospray needle and/or a gas pressure to push the sample through the needle. See also electrospray ionization and microelectrospray.Ionization that occurs through the interaction of two or more neutral gaseous species, at least one of which is internally excited.The ionization of material in a solid sample by bombarding it with ionic or neutral atoms formed as a result of the fission of a suitable nuclide, typically 252Cf. Synonymous with fission fragment ionization.OBSOLETE An electronic state capable of undergoing auto-Ionization.The process in which ions are ejected from a sample surface as a result of bombardment by a primary beam of atoms or ions.The formation of gas-phase ions without extensive fragmentation.The formation of ions from a solid material by an intermittent electrical discharge.The formation of gas-phase ions from molecules that are deposited on a particular surface substrate that is irradiated with a pulsed laser. See also matrix-assisted laser desorption ionization.The ionization of a neutral species when it interacts with a solid surface with an appropriate work function and temperature.The ionization of a neutral species through contact with a high temperature surface.A process in which an electron is removed from or added to a molecule without a change in the positions of the atoms. The resulting ion is typically in an excited vibrational state.OBSOLETE The reaction of an ion with a neutral species in which the reactants combine to form a single ion.OBSOLETE A homolytic cleavage where the bond fission occurs between the atom adjacent to the atom at the apparent charge site and an atom removed from the apparent charge site by two bonds.OBSOLETE A homolytic cleavage where the bond fission occurs between an atom removed from the apparent charge site atom by two bonds and an atom adjacent to that atom and removed from the apparent charge site by three bonds.An inert gas used for collisional deactivation of internally excited ions.OBSOLETE Fragmentation of an odd electron ion in which the cleaved bond is adjacent to the apparent charge site. Synonymous with charge mediated fragmentation.OBSOLETE Reaction of an ion with a neutral species in which the charge on the product ion is reversed in sign with respect to the reactant ion.OBSOLETE The reaction of an ion with a neutral species with a resulting change in the magnitude or sign of the charge on the reactant ion.OBSOLETE Reaction of a positive ion with a neutral species in which the positive charge on the product ion is greater than that on the reactant ion.OBSOLETE The reaction of an ion with a neutral species in which some or all of the charge of the reactant ion is transferred to the neutral species.OBSOLETE The reaction of an ion with a neutral species in which the translational energy of the collision is converted into internal energy of the ion.An inert gas used for collisional excitation. The term target gas is not recommended.OBSOLETE Fragmentation of a molecule or ion in which both electrons forming the single bond that is broken remain on one of the atoms that were originally bonded. This term is synonymous with heterolysis.OBSOLETE Collision-induced dissociation process wherein the projectile ion has laboratory-frame translational energy higher than 1 keV.A collision-induced dissociation process that occurs in a beam-type collision cell.OBSOLETE Fragmentation of an odd electron ion that results from one of a pair of electrons that form a bond between two atoms moving to form a pair with the odd electron on the atom at the apparent charge site. Fragmentation results in the formation of an even electron ion and a radical. This reaction involves the movement of a single electron and is symbolized by a single-barbed arrow. Synonymous with Homolysis.OBSOLETE Exchange of hydrogen atoms with deuterium atoms in a molecule or pre-formed ion in solution prior to introduction into a mass spectrometer, or by reaction of an ion with a deuterated collision gas inside a mass spectrometer.OBSOLETE A plot of the relative abundance of a beam or other collection of ions as a function their loss of translational energy in reactions with neutral species.OBSOLETE The reaction of an ion with a neutral species in which one or more electrons are removed from either the ion or neutral.OBSOLETE The reaction of an ion with a neutral molecule. The term ion-molecule reaction is not recommended because the hyphen suggests a single species that is that is both an ion and a molecule.OBSOLETE A particular type of transition state that lies between precursor and product ions on the reaction coordinate of some ion reactions.OBSOLETE A process wherein a charged species interacts with a neutral reactant to produce either chemically different species or changes in the internal energy of one or both of the reactants.OBSOLETE In this reaction an association reaction is accompanied by the subsequent or simultaneous liberation of a different neutral species as a product.OBSOLETE An approach to determination of ion thermodynamic quantities by a bracketing procedure in which the relative probabilities of competing ion fragmentations are measured via the relative abundances of the reaction products. The extended kinetic method takes the associated entropy changes into account.OBSOLETE A collision between an ion and neutral species with translational energy approximately 1000 eV or lower.A collision-induced dissociation process wherein the precursor ion has the translational energy lower than approximately 1000 eV. This process typically requires multiple collisions and the collisional excitation is cumulative.OBSOLETE A dissociation reaction triggered by transfer of a hydrogen atom via a 6-member transition state to the formal radical/charge site from a carbon atom four atoms removed from the charge/radical site (the gamma-carbon); subsequent rearrangement of electron density leads to expulsion of an olefin molecule. This term was originally applied to ketone ions where the charge/radical site is the carbonyl oxygen, but it is now more widely applied.A process wherein the reactant ion is dissociated as a result of absorption of one or more photons.OBSOLETE Reaction of an ion with a neutral species in which some but not all of the ion charge is transferred to the neutral.OBSOLETE Chemical transformation involving an ion.OBSOLETE Collision in which the translational energy of the fast-moving collision partner is increased at the expense of internal energy of one or both collision partners.OBSOLETE A process wherein a reactant ion interacts with a surface to produce either chemically different species or a change in the internal energy of the reactant ion.OBSOLETE Fragmentation reaction in which the molecularity is treated as one, irrespective of whether the dissociative state is that of a metastable ion produced in the ion source or results from collisional excitation of a stable ion.Function or process of the mass spectrometer where it records a spectrum.Representation of intensity values corresponding to a range of measurement space.Mass analyzer separates the ions according to their mass-to-charge ratio.OBSOLETE Mass/charge separation Method.OBSOLETE Sequential m/z separation method.The ionization of any species by the interaction of a focused beam of ions having a translational energy of several thousand eV with a solid sample.Finnigan LTQ MS.Finnigan LTQ FT MS.Finnigan LTQ Orbitrap MS.Finnigan LXQ MS.Terms used to describe the Analyzer.Terms used to describe types of data processing.The device that detects ions.OBSOLETE Additional terms to describe the instrument as outlined in the mass spec doc, Appendix 1, section 1.5.Ion selection properties that are associated with a value.Terms to describe the precursor activation.Terms to describe the sample.Terms to describe the source.OBSOLETE Terms used to describe the spectrum.OBSOLETE Terms to describe units.OBSOLETE Terms to describe Additional.Device used in the construction of a mass spectrometer to focus, contain or otherwise manipulate ions.Description of the instrument or the mass spectrometer.OBSOLETE A unit of measurement for mass.Relative orientation of the electromagnetic field during the selection and detection of ions in the mass spectrometer.OBSOLETE Alternating.The 1200 Series LC/MSD SL ion trap belongs to the Agilent LC/MSD ion trap family. It provides fast polarity switching and multisignal data acquisition capabilities in a single run while also providing 5 stages of automated data dependent MS2 and 11 stages of manual MS2.The 6110 Quadrupole LC/MS system is a Agilent liquid chromatography instrument combined with an entry level single quadrupole mass spectrometer from the 6100 Series of Agilent quadrupole mass spectrometers. 6110 Quadrupole mass spectrometer has m/z range of 10-1500 and 2500 u/s scan speed. It proves useful for wide range of SIM quantitative applications.The 6120A Quadrupole LC/MS system is a Agilent liquid chromatography instrument combined with a single quadrupole mass spectrometer from the 6100 Series of Agilent mass spectrometers. 6120 quadrupole mass spectrometer has m/z range of 10-1500, 2500 u/s scan speed and utilizes multiple signal acquisition.The 6130 Quadrupole LC/MS system is a Agilent liquid chromatography instrument combined with a single quadrupole mass spectrometer from the 6100 series of Agilent mass spectrometers. The 6130 quadrupole mass spectrometer has m/z range of 2-3000, 2500 u/s scan speed in standard mode and 5250 u/s speed in fast-scan mode. It also uses multiple signal acquisition.The 6140 Quadrupole LC/MS system is a Agilent liquid chromatography instrument combined with a single quadrupole mass spectrometer from the 6100 Series of Agilent quadrupole mass spectrometers. 6140 Quadrupole mass spectrometer has m/z range of 10-1350, 2500 u/s scan speed in standard mode and 10000 u/s speed in fast-scan mode. It also uses multiple signal acquisition.The 6210 Time-of-Flight LC/MS is a Agilent liquid chromatography instrument combined with a Agilent time of flight mass spectrometer. This time of flight mass spectrometer has a m/z range of 50-12000, mass accuracy of less than 2 ppm and resolution greater than 13,000 at m/z 2722. It has multiple ion sources and can be used with multimode ion sources.The 6310 Ion Trap LC/MS is a Agilent liquid chromatography instrument combined with a 6300 series Agilent ion trap. It has a mass range of 50-2200 between 0.6 to 0.35 resolution and mass range of 200-4000 with resolution of 3-4. The scan speed varies from 1650-27000 for the respective mass ranges.The 6320 Ion Trap LC/MS is a Agilent liquid chromatography instrument combined with a 6300 series Agilent ion trap. It has a mass range of 50-2200 between 0.6 to 0.25 resolution and mass range of 200-4000 with resolution of less than 3. The scan speed varies from 1650-27000 for the respective mass ranges.The 6330 Ion Trap LC/MS is a Agilent liquid chromatography instrument combined with a 6300 series Agilent ion trap. It has a mass range of 50-2200 between 0.6 to 0.25 resolution and mass range of 200-4000 with resolution of less than 3. The scan speed varies from 1650-27000 for the respective mass ranges.The 6340 Ion Trap LC/MS is a Agilent liquid chromatography instrument combined with a 6300 series Agilent ion trap. It has a mass range of 50-2200 between 0.6 to 0.25 resolution and mass range of 200-4000 with resolution of less than 3. The scan speed varies from 1650-27000 for the respective mass ranges.The 6410 Quadrupole LC/MS system is a Agilent liquid chromatography instrument combined with a Agilent triple quadrupole mass spectrometer. Mass range of the mass spectrometer is 15-1650 m/z, resolution is at three settings of 0.7 u (unit), 1.2 u (wide) and 2.5 u (widest). The mass accuracy for 6410 mass spectrometer is 0.1 across the mass range. The collision cell is a hexapole with linear acceleration.The LC/MSD VL ion trap is part of the family of Agilent ion trap mass spectrometers. It has ESI, APCI and APPI ion sources and is a useful ion trap when the amount of sample is not the limiting factor.OBSOLETE Terms that will likely become obsolete unless there are wails of dissent.Analyzer properties that are associated with a value.Detector attribute recognized as a value.Property of a source device that need a value.Thermo Fisher Scientific instrument model. The company has gone through several names including Thermo Finnigan, Thermo Scientific.An ion trapping device that consists of an outer barrel-like electrode and a coaxial inner spindle-like electrode that form an electrostatic field with quadro-logarithmic potential distribution. The frequency of harmonic oscillations of the orbitally trapped ions along the axis of the electrostatic field is independent of the ion velocity and is inversely proportional to the square root of m/z so that the trap can be used as a mass analyzer.Nanospray Inlet.Potential difference at the MS source in volts.Ion optics involves components that help focus ion streams in mass spectrometry.Hitachi instrument model.Varian instrument model.Agilent instrument model.Dionex instrument model.Thermo Electron Corporation instrument model.Finnigan MAT instrument model.Thermo Scientific instrument model.Applied Biosystems instrument model.Instrument properties that are associated with a value.Special scan mode where data with improved resolution is acquired. This is typically achieved by scanning a more narrow m/z window or scanning with a lower scan rate.OBSOLETE Feature of the ion trap mass spectrometer where MS data is acquired over a mass range.Nonphysical characteristic attributed to a spectrum.The upper m/z bound of a mass spectrometer scan window.The lower m/z bound of a mass spectrometer scan window.The time spent gathering data across a peak.Nonphysical characteristic attributed to a spectrum acquisition scan.M/z value of the signal of highest intensity in the mass spectrum.The intensity of the greatest peak in the mass spectrum.OBSOLETE Ion Role.Nonphysical characteristic attributed to an ion.OBSOLETE Ion Type.Activation Energy.Precursor Activation Attribute.Stage number achieved in a multi stage mass spectrometry acquisition.A string unique to Thermo instrument describing instrument settings for the scan.A data array of values.A data array of m/z values.A data array of intensity values.A data array of charge values.A data array of signal-to-noise values.Encoding type of binary data specifying the binary representation and precision, e.g. 64-bit float.Signed 32-bit little-endian integer.OBSOLETE Signed 16-bit float.32-bit precision little-endian floating point conforming to IEEE-754.Signed 64-bit little-endian integer.64-bit precision little-endian floating point conforming to IEEE-754.Describes the data content on the file.Way in which the spectrum is represented, either with regularly spaced data points or with a list of centroided peaks.Waters data file format found in a Waters RAW directory, generated from an MS acquisition.Highest m/z value observed in the m/z array.Lowest m/z value observed in the m/z array.Serial Number of the instrument.Conversion of one file format to another.Software related to the recording or transformation of spectra.Thermo Finnigan software for data acquisition and analysis.Thermo Finnigan software for data analysis of peptides and proteins.Micromass software for data acquisition and analysis.Bruker software for data analysis.Applied Biosystems software for data acquisition and analysis.Applied Biosystems software for data acquisition and analysis.A software for converting Waters raw directory format to mzXML or mzML. MassWolf was originally developed at the Institute for Systems Biology.Applied Biosystems MALDI-TOF data acquisition and analysis system.Bruker software for data acquisition.A software program for converting Thermo Finnigan RAW file format to mzXML or mzML. ReAdW was originally developed at the Institute for Systems Biology. Its whimsical interleaved spelling and capitalization is pronounced \"readraw\".A software program for converting Applied Biosystems wiff file format to mzXML format. MzStar was originally developed at the Institute for Systems Biology. It is now obsoleted by the MzWiff program.Data processing attribute used to describe the type of data processing performed on the data file.Conversion of a file format to Proteomics Standards Initiative mzML file format.Conversion of a file format to Institute of Systems Biology mzXML file format.Conversion of a file format to Proteomics Standards Initiative mzData file format.Object Attribute.Sample properties that are associated with a value.Selection window properties that are associated with a value.OBSOLETE Time Unit.SCIEX or Applied Biosystems|MDS SCIEX software for data acquisition.OBSOLETE Maldi Spot Identifier.A software program for converting Agilent MassHunter format to mzXML or mzML. Trapper was originally developed at the Institute for Systems Biology.ThermoFinnigan LCQ Deca.LTQ Orbitrap Discovery.LTQ Orbitrap XL.LTQ FT Ultra.GC Quantum.Spectrum type.The format of the file being used. This could be a instrument or vendor specific proprietary file format or a converted open file format.Checksum is a form of redundancy check, a simple way to protect the integrity of data by detecting errors in data.Applied Biosystems WIFF file format.Thermo Scientific RAW file format.Proteomics Standards Initiative mzData file format.Micromass PKL file format.Institute of Systems Biology mzXML file format.Bruker/Agilent YEP file format.MD5 (Message-Digest algorithm 5) is a (now deprecated) cryptographic hash function with a 128-bit hash value used to check the integrity of files.SHA-1 (Secure Hash Algorithm-1) is a cryptographic hash function designed by the National Security Agency (NSA). It is also used to verify file integrity. Since 2011 it has been deprecated by the NIST as a U. S. government standard.Method used to combine the mass spectra.Spectra Sum.Compression Type.Spectra is combined by calculating the median of the spectra.Zlib.Spectra is combined by calculating the mean of the spectra.No Compression.Data file from which an entity is sourced.LCQ Fleet.Mass spectrum created by a single-stage MS experiment or the first stage of a multi-stage experiment.MSn refers to multi-stage MS2 experiments designed to record product ion spectra where n is the number of product ion stages (progeny ions). For ion traps, sequential MS/MS experiments can be undertaken where n > 2 whereas for a simple triple quadrupole system n=2. Use the term ms level (MS:1000511) for specifying n.Spectrum generated from MSn experiment with three or more stages of m/z separation and in which a particular multi-step reaction path is monitored.Spectrum obtained with the operation of a mass spectrometer in which the abundances of one ion or several ions of specific m/z values are recorded rather than the entire mass spectrum (Selected Ion Monitoring).Spectrum obtained when data are acquired from specific product ions corresponding to m/z values of selected precursor ions a recorded via two or more stages of mass spectrometry. The precursor/product ion pair is called a transition pair. Data can be obtained for a single transition pair or multiple transition pairs. Multiple time segments of different transition pairs can exist in a single file. Single precursor ions can have multiple product ions consitituting multiple transition pairs. Selected reaction monitoring can be performed as tandem mass spectrometry in time or tandem mass spectrometry in space.Proteomics Standards Initiative mzML file format.Details about a person or organization to contact in case of concern or discussion about the file.Name of the contact person or organization.Postal address of the contact person or organization.Uniform Resource Locator related to the contact person or organization.Email address of the contact person or organization.Home institution of the contact person.A software program for converting Applied Biosystems wiff file format to the mzXML or mzML format. MzWiff is currently maintained at the Institute for Systems Biology. It replaces the slower mzStar program.A process of reducing spikes of intensity in order to reduce noise while preserving real peak signal. Many algorithms can be applied for this process.A process of removal of varying intensities generated due to variable energy absorption before further processing can take place. Baseline reduction facilitates meaningful comparison between intensities of m/z values.The removal of very low intensity data points that are likely to be spurious noise rather than real signal.A data array of relative time offset values from a reference time.An attribute of resolution when recording the detector response in absence of the analyte.The electrical potential used to impart kinetic energy to ions in a mass spectrometer.A process to fragment ions in a mass spectrometer by inducing fragmentation of cations (e.g. peptides or proteins) by transferring electrons from radical-anions.A process that involves precursor ion activation at high Q, a time delay to allow the precursor to fragment, then a rapid pulse to low Q where all fragment ions are trapped. The product ions can then be scanned out of the ion trap and detected.Database application and analysis platform for proteomics.Waters software for data analysis.Shimadzu MALDI-TOF instrument model.Shimadzu Scientific Instruments instrument model.Shimadzu Scientific Instruments LCMS-IT-TOF MS.Shimadzu Scientific Instruments LCMS-2010EV MS.Shimadzu Scientific Instruments LCMS-2010A MS.Shimadzu Biotech AXIMA CFR MALDI-TOF MS.Shimadzu Biotech AXIMA-QIT MS.Shimadzu Biotech AXIMA-CFR plus MS.Shimadzu Biotech AXIMA Performance MALDI-TOF/TOF MS.Shimadzu Biotech AXIMA Confidence MALDI-TOF (curved field reflectron) MS.Shimadzu Biotech AXIMA Assurance Linear MALDI-TOF MS.SEQUEST DTA file format.Peak list file format used by ProteinLynx Global Server.ProteoWizard software for data processing and analysis. Primarily developed by the labs of P. Malick and D. Tabb.A user-defined scan configuration that specifies the instrumental settings in which a spectrum is acquired. An instrument may cycle through a list of preset scan configurations to acquire data. This is a more generic term for the Thermo \"scan event\", which is defined in the Thermo Xcalibur glossary as: a mass spectrometer scan that is defined by choosing the necessary scan parameter settings. Multiple scan events can be defined for each segment of time.A data array of electromagnetic radiation wavelength values.Highest wavelength observed in an EMR spectrum.Lowest wavelength observed in an EMR spectrum.OBSOLETE Spectrum generated from a photodiode array detector (ultraviolet/visible spectrum).An array detector used to record spectra in the ultraviolet and visible region of light.Surveyor 