a-z0-9A-Z]+).A unit of measurement is a standardized quantity of a physical quality.A unit which is a standard measure of the distance between two points.A unit which is a standard measure of the amount of matter/energy of a physical object.A unit which is a standard measure of the dimension in which events occur in sequence.A unit which is a standard measure of the average kinetic energy of the particles in a sample of matter.A length unit which is equal to the length of the path traveled by light in vacuum during a time interval of 1/299 792 458 of a second.A time unit which is equal to the duration of 9 192 631 770 periods of the radiation corresponding to the transition between the two hyperfine levels of the ground state of the caesium 133 atom.A thermodynamic temperature unit which is equal to the fraction 1/273.16 of the thermodynamic temperature of the triple point of water.A length unit which is equal to one thousandth of a meter or 10^[-3] m.A length unit which is equal to one millionth of a meter or 10^[-6] m.A length unit which is equal to one thousandth of one millionth of a meter or 10^[-9] m.A length unit which is equal to 10 [-10] m.A mass unit which is equal to one thousandth of a kilogram or 10^[-3] kg.A temperature unit which is equal to one kelvin degree. However, they have their zeros at different points. The centigrade scale has its zero at 273.15 K.A time unit which is equal to one thousandth of a second or 10^[-3] s.A time unit which is equal to 60 seconds.A unit which is one of a particular measure to which all measures of that type can be related.A unit which is a standard measure of the amount of a 2-dimensional flat surface.A density unit which is a standard measure of the mass of a substance in a given volume.A unit which is a standard measure of the amount of space occupied by any substance, whether solid, liquid, or gas.A volume unit which is equal to one thousandth of a liter or 10^[-3] L, or to 1 cubic centimeter.A unit which is a standard measure of the number of repetitive actions in a particular time.A frequency unit which is equal to 1 complete cycle of a recurring phenomenon in 1 second.A unit which is a standard measure of the force applied to a given area.A pressure unit which is equal to the pressure or stress on a surface caused by a force of 1 newton spread over a surface of 1 m^[2].A unit which is a standard measure of the work done by a certain force (gravitational, electric, magnetic, force of inertia, etc).An energy unit which is equal to the energy required when a force of 1 newton moves an object 1 meter in the direction of the force.A unit which is a standard measure of the figure or space formed by the junction of two lines or planes.A unit which is a standard measure of the angle formed by two straight lines in the same plane.A time unit which is equal to one thousandth of one millionth of a second or 10^[-9] s.A dimensionless concentration notation which describes the amount of one substance in another. It is the ratio of the amount of the substance of interest to the amount of that substance plus the amount of the substance.A dimensionless concentration notation which denotes the amount of a given substance in a total amount of 1,000,000 regardless of the units of measure used as long as they are the same or 1 part in 10^[6].A mass unit density which is equal to mass of an object in grams divided by the volume in liters.A unit which is a standard measure of the influence exerted by some mass.A plane angle unit which is equal to 1/360 of a full rotation or 1.7453310^[-2] rad.A unit which is a standard measure of physical quantity consisting of only a numerical number without any units.A dimensionless ratio unit which denotes numbers as fractions of 100.A dimensionless unit which denotes a simple count of things.A dimensionless unit which denotes an amount or magnitude of one quantity relative to another.A dimensionless ratio unit which relates the part (the numerator) to the whole (the denominator).A unit which is a standard measure of the work done per unit charge as a charge is moved between two points in an electric field.An electric potential difference unit which is equal to the work per unit charge. One volt is the potential difference required to move one coulomb of charge between two points in a circuit while using one joule of energy.An independently to the base SI units defined mass unit which is equal to one twelfth of the mass of an unbound atom of the carbon-12 nuclide, at rest and in its ground state.A mass unit which is equal to one thousand daltons.A unit which is a standard measure of the strength of a magnetic field.A magnetic flux density unit which is equal to one weber per square meter.A non-SI unit of energy (eV) defined as the energy acquired by a single unbound electron when it passes through an electrostatic potential difference of one volt. An electronvolt is equal to 1.602 176 53(14) x 10^-19 J.The electric field strength is a unit which is a measure of the potential difference between two points some distance apart.The volt per meter is a unit of electric field strength equal to the a potential difference of 1 volt existing between two points that are 1 meter apart.A dimensionless logarithmic unit assigned to a measure of absorbance of light through a partially absorbing substance, defined as -log10(I/I_0) where I = transmitted light and I_0 = incident light.A unit which is a standard measure of the volume of fluid which passes through a given surface per unit time .A volumetric flow rate unit which is equal to one microliter volume through a given surface in one minute.An area unit which is equal to an area enclosed by a square with sides each 1 angstrom long.",
